      <h1>What Is Microneedling? <em>The Skin Treatment You May Be Able to Claim</em></h1>
      <p>You may know microneedling as a spa treatment or a social media staple. In a clinic, the same treatment can be part of naturopathic care, and that difference can matter for your benefits.</p>
      <p class="byline">By WellFinder · November 2026 · 6 min read</p>
    </div>
      </div>
    
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="article-wrap">
        <div class="article-body">

          <p>You lie back, a pen-like device moves across your skin in small sections, and you leave looking a bit pink.</p>
          <p>A few days later, the redness settles. Over the weeks that follow, your skin may start to look smoother.</p>
          <p>That is microneedling in a nutshell. It is offered everywhere from medi-spas to naturopathic clinics, and the treatment itself is much the same in both places.</p>
          <p>What can be very different is who provides it, and how it is billed. That distinction can matter if you are hoping to use your Canadian extended health benefits.</p>

          <figure class="article-figure">
            <img src="/assets/clinics/health-globe-wellness-clinic-v2.jpg" alt="A microneedling pen being used on a client's cheek during a treatment" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Microneedling at <a href="/clinics-microneedling">Health Globe Wellness Clinic</a>, Toronto</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2>So, what exactly is microneedling?</h2>
          <p>Microneedling is a skin treatment that uses a device with very fine needles. It is sometimes called collagen induction therapy.</p>
          <p>The device, often a pen, is moved across the skin and creates thousands of tiny, controlled punctures. These are sometimes called micro-injuries. (Singh and Yadav, Indian Dermatology Online Journal)</p>
          <p>Your skin responds the way it responds to any small injury. It gets to work repairing itself, releasing growth factors and laying down new collagen and elastin, the proteins that give skin its firmness and smoothness.</p>
          <p>That new collagen is the point of the treatment. It builds gradually, underneath the surface, over the weeks and months after a session.</p>
          <p>People commonly look into microneedling for:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Acne scars</li>
            <li>Fine lines and wrinkles</li>
            <li>Uneven skin texture</li>
            <li>Enlarged pores</li>
            <li>Dark spots and uneven pigmentation</li>
            <li>Stretch marks</li>
          </ul>
          <p>(American Academy of Dermatology)</p>
          <p>Unlike some resurfacing treatments, microneedling does not remove layers of skin. The surface is punctured, not stripped away, which is part of why the recovery is usually short.</p>

          <h2>What happens during your first assessment?</h2>
          <p>Your first appointment is usually more than walking in and lying down.</p>
          <p>Your practitioner will typically ask about your skin concerns, your health history, any medications or supplements you take, and treatments you have had before.</p>
          <p>Then they will look closely at your skin. Part of the assessment is checking whether anything should delay treatment.</p>
          <p>Microneedling is usually postponed if there is an active skin infection, inflamed acne in the treatment area, or a recent tan or sunburn. Certain medications, including some acne medications, are also a reason to wait. Tell your practitioner about anything you are taking, even if it seems unrelated. (American Academy of Dermatology; Cleveland Clinic)</p>
          <p>From there, your practitioner decides whether microneedling is suitable for you, and if so, builds a plan: which areas to treat, what depth to use, and how many sessions to start with.</p>
          <p>If your practitioner is a naturopathic doctor, the first visit may be a longer appointment. Naturopathic doctors typically take a detailed health history before recommending any treatment. (Canadian Association of Naturopathic Doctors)</p>
          <p>The goal of the assessment is not to sell you the longest plan. It is to match the treatment to your skin, and to be honest when microneedling is not the right tool.</p>

          <h2>What does a microneedling session look like?</h2>
          <p>On the day, your skin is cleansed and a numbing cream is applied. The cream is left on for a while, often around 45 minutes, so the treatment itself is more comfortable. (Singh and Yadav, Indian Dermatology Online Journal)</p>
          <p>Once your skin is numb, the practitioner moves the pen across the treatment area in sections. The needling itself often takes about 10 to 20 minutes for an area such as the face.</p>
          <p>A small amount of pinpoint bleeding can be normal during treatment. It settles quickly.</p>
          <p>Afterwards, a moisturizer or soothing product is applied, and you will usually be reminded to use sunscreen and protect the treated skin while it heals.</p>
          <p>Expect your skin to look red and feel a little swollen or sunburned afterwards. For most people this lasts a few days. Many people are back to their normal routine within a day, even if some pinkness lingers. (Cleveland Clinic)</p>
          <p>Your clinic will give you aftercare instructions. Follow them, and ask when to restart makeup and your usual active skincare products.</p>
          <p>Some clinics also offer microneedling combined with platelet-rich plasma, or PRP, which is prepared from a small sample of your own blood and applied during the treatment. It is an add-on, and it adds to the price.</p>

          <h2>How many sessions do people usually need?</h2>
          <p>One session is rarely the whole story.</p>
          <p>Collagen takes time to build. Reviews of the treatment describe multiple sittings, spaced about three to eight weeks apart, with results continuing to develop for three to six months after a series ends. (Singh and Yadav, Indian Dermatology Online Journal; Cleveland Clinic)</p>
          <p>For acne scars, the American Academy of Dermatology gives an example of three to five treatments, spaced two to four weeks apart.</p>
          <p>So a reasonable starting expectation is a series of roughly three to five sessions, spaced a few weeks apart, rather than a single visit. Deeper scarring or more stubborn concerns can take more.</p>
          <p>Some people then do a maintenance session about once a year to keep up their results. (Cleveland Clinic)</p>
          <p>Treat these numbers as a starting point, not a promise. Your practitioner will confirm the plan at your assessment, based on your skin and what you are hoping to improve.</p>

          <h2>What does microneedling cost in Canada?</h2>
          <p>Prices vary widely, even within the same city. These are per-session prices from clinic listings verified for the WellFinder directory:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Qi Integrated Health, Vancouver: $248–$375</li>
            <li>Dr. Caitlin Batting, Calgary: $280</li>
            <li>Dr. Lauren Cottom, Winnipeg: $400</li>
            <li>Well BYND, Toronto: $450</li>
            <li>Everwell ND, Burlington: $695, including PRP</li>
          </ul>
          <p>So a single session in Canada commonly falls somewhere between about $250 and $700.</p>
          <p>Where your price lands depends on the clinic, the city, the size of the area treated, and add-ons. PRP is the big one: it can add hundreds of dollars to a session, as the Everwell ND price shows.</p>
          <p>And because most people do a series, the total cost adds up quickly. That is worth knowing before you start, and it is one reason to check the benefits question below before booking your first session.</p>

          <figure class="article-figure">
            <img src="/assets/clinics/qi-integrated-health.jpg" alt="The treatment rooms inside Qi Integrated Health in Vancouver" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>Inside <a href="/clinics-microneedling">Qi Integrated Health</a>, Vancouver</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2>Can microneedling be claimed through your benefits?</h2>
          <p>This is where you should slow down before booking.</p>
          <p>Microneedling is not automatically an insurance benefit just because it takes place in a clinic.</p>
          <p>If a registered naturopathic doctor provides the treatment as part of naturopathic care, the appointment may be eligible under your naturopathy coverage, depending on your plan. Many extended health plans in Canada include naturopathic services, with annual maximums and per-visit limits. (Canadian Association of Naturopathic Doctors)</p>
          <p>The same treatment at a spa, or provided by an esthetician, is a personal care service. It is generally not billable to extended health benefits, no matter how clinical the room looks.</p>
          <p>For your benefits, what matters is the practitioner's licence and what appears on your receipt.</p>
          <p>Naturopathic doctors in regulated provinces complete a four-year, doctoral-level naturopathic medical program after undergraduate study, and register with a provincial regulator. You can confirm a practitioner's registration on the regulator's public register before you book. (Canadian Association of Naturopathic Doctors)</p>
          <p>Your safest approach is to ask both the clinic and your insurer.</p>
          <p>Before booking, ask:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Who will provide my treatment?</li>
            <li>What profession will the service be billed under?</li>
            <li>What will appear on my receipt?</li>
            <li>Is this specific service eligible under my plan?</li>
            <li>Do you offer direct billing?</li>
          </ul>
          <p>You do not need a referral to book with a naturopathic doctor. You can contact the clinic directly. (Canadian Association of Naturopathic Doctors)</p>
          <p>Direct billing also does not guarantee that your plan will pay for the full appointment.</p>

          <h2>Is microneedling right for you?</h2>
          <p>Microneedling may be worth exploring if acne scars, uneven texture, or early fine lines are your main concern, and you are comfortable with a series of treatments rather than a one-off fix.</p>
          <p>It is probably not the right week for it if you have an active breakout or skin infection, or a big event in the next few days. Your skin needs a little time to settle after each session.</p>
          <p>You do not have to decide alone. A good practitioner will tell you if microneedling suits your skin, suggest something else if it does not, and give you a realistic sense of how many sessions to expect.</p>

          <h2>Find microneedling clinics</h2>
          <p>If you are curious about microneedling and want to see what is available near you, start by comparing clinics and asking about their practitioners, services, and insurance process.</p>

